WEBVTT

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:28.480
Leave it to Beaver, starring Barbara Billingsley, Hugh Beaumont, Tony Dow, Jerry Mathers as the Beaver.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:05.250
Ward?

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:07.530
Hi, dear.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:08.070
Hi.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:09.760
What's going on out there?

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.380
Tui and Chester came over.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.500
It seems they're building something with Wally and Beaver.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:17.830
Oh, well, they could be building with barrel staves.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:19.730
Well, they were very mysterious about it

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:21.150
when they came in for milk and cookies.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.010
I think the project's off limits for adults.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.330
Well, I guess we'll just have to respect their privacy

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:27.430
till something blows up.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.910
They've been working out there since they got home from

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.980
Aren't you a little bit curious?

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:33.110
No, not particularly.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:34.840
I think they're using your tools.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.240
Uh, maybe after I change clothes I will just wander out

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.220
and see what's going on.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:43.580
Thought you would.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.760
Chester, we can use this one for the middle.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:53.260
Okay.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:55.390
How are you doing, Joey?

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.280
Beaver, will you get your head out of our kayak?

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:03.220
Okay.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:11.170
Are you sure this is the kind of boat the Eskimos use?

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.850
Well, sure, Beaver. You saw it in the movie last Saturday.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.670
They hunt walruses and polar bears and everything in boats like this.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.220
But where do they get barrel hoops at the North Pole?

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:24.200
Wally, does your brother have to hang around and ask stupid questions?

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.000
Yeah, Beaver, cut it out, will you?

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:29.920
You know, we can try it out at Miller's Pond.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:31.680
before we take it in the ocean.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.420
Hey, Chester, how come we couldn't work on it at your place anymore?

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.900
Oh, my father said we were messing up his garage.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.340
He said to bring it over here.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:41.590
Hi, Mr. Cleaver. Oh, hi, Dad.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:42.850
Hi, Dad. Hello, fellas.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.780
Hey, Dad, do you think you could leave the car out tonight?

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.150
Well, if we move this thing, it's liable to fall apart.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:52.410
Well, I think you could move it over to the side, couldn't you?

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:53.990
Hey, Wally, hold it steady, will you?

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.970
Um, what is it? A racing car?

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.730
No, Dad, it's a genuine Eskimo boat.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.150
Yeah, Mr. Cleaver, we're gonna try it in Miller's Pond,

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:06.030
then take it in the ocean.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:11.140
Well, you've, uh, got quite a lot of play in there, haven't you, fella?

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.220
Oh, we're gonna cover the whole thing with chicken wire.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:15.580
Yeah, and then we're gonna cover the outside with skin.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:19.180
Uh-huh. Well, be sure you have plenty of weight down by the keel.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:20.640
You need a low center of gravity.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:24.230
Heck, we figured the guy that gets in it will be the center of gravity.

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:29.940
Fine. Well, uh, don't be discouraged, fella.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.560
This is the way Robert Fulton started.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.120
Be sure to put my tools away.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:38.320
Hey, Wally, I think your dad's giving us a business about Robert Fulton.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.360
Nah, that just means he thinks we're goofy trying to build a boat,

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:43.720
but he just doesn't want to come right out and say it.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.620
Did you find out what the mysterious project is?

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:51.230
Yeah, they're building a boat.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.380
They're going to try it out in Miller's pond

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:55.760
before they take it out in the ocean.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.360
Oh, boy, that sounds dangerous.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:03:58.940
No.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:03:59.980
First place, the other place.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.980
ocean 20 miles away in the second place they'll abandon the project before the weekend and finally

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:12.280
you just can't build a boat with barrel hoops and wire i hope not i wish you'd keep an eye on them

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:17.030
though hey dad watch those tanks we got a couple weeks ago go down underneath them right near the

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:27.640
drawer um say wally uh i know you fellas are having a lot of fun out there but i think before

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.960
you waste a lot of time you ought to realize that you just

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.760
can't slap together a mess like that and expect it to float well gee dad you're always telling us

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.940
to build something worthwhile and now that we're building it you're saying it's no good all right

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:45.940
well you go ahead and build your boat i just want one thing perfectly clear if you finish the

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:57.620
boat i don't want you doing anything reckless or foolish with it okay okay dad okay okay i'd

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:59.980
feel a lot better if they weren't building a boat at all.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.020
I thought we would.

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.780
Yeah, it's beginning to look like something now.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:10.750
Hey, we can't fasten anything to the sides.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:12.350
What are we gonna use for a seat?

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:15.980
Well, I'll look around the house later and see what we got.

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:18.370
Maybe we could use a breadboard or something.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:21.270
What have you been doing down in the cellar?

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.220
I was looking for that bucket I used to wash the car with.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:25.120
I can't find it anywhere.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:27.260
Well, I haven't seen it since last summer

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:28.840
when the boys made lemonade in it.

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:29.860
Oh, turn it.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:30.520
Here, I'll take this.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:31.240
Thank you.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:34.180
Lemonade.

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:36.630
Man, that's neat.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:37.050
Yeah.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:39.790
Hey, Wally, did your paw-paller too much for taking this bucket?

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:40.420
Nah.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:41.760
All he did was look at me.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:42.240
You know.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:44.940
Well, now all we gotta do is get some skin to cover the boat.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:47.680
There's no animals around here that got that much skin.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.340
I guess the most use seal skin.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:50.560
What are we gonna use?

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:53.600
Hey, my father's got an old raccoon coat.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:54.740
Maybe we could use that.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:55.860
Aw, cut it out.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:57.280
Who wants a fuzzy canoe?

00:05:58.550 --> 00:05:58.910
What?

00:05:59.290 --> 00:05:59.570
Yeah.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:01.100
Paper just came.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:01.480
Oh.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:05.320
Well, I noticed the boat builders were still hard at work when I came home.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:06.480
I couldn't get in the garage.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:10.580
They even borrowed three of those old oilcloth table covers we had around.

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:12.560
What would they want with tablecloths?

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.300
Maybe they're getting ready for a launching picnic.

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:29.820
You all, these tablecloths of your mobs work real neat.

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:33.310
Yeah, keep sloppin' the varnish on. It'll keep it from leakin'.

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:36.900
Hey, guys, if the varnish is dry, we can try it out over at Miller's Pond tomorrow.

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:39.690
Um, well, gee, I don't know.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.100
Maybe we should ask my dad to come along.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.720
What would you want to do that for? He'd spoil all our fun.

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:49.970
Yeah, well, I guess I want her just to see if it floats.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:55.110
And hey, Wally, will you tell this little squirt not to come taggin' along tomorrow?

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:57.750
Well, I can go to Miller's Pond if I wanna.

00:06:58.110 --> 00:06:59.130
You better not wanna.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:06:59.980
I can't.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:06.980
Well, I guess you can come along and watch us, if you wanna.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:11.150
Wart!

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:12.100
Yeah?

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:14.970
Oh, Wart, I just looked out in the garage.

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:15.910
The boat's gone.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:18.920
I knew when those boys got up early they were up to something.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:21.220
Well, I've been after Wally to get it out of there.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:22.660
He probably took it back over to Chester's.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:23.830
Oh.

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:26.270
I'll mess up the Anderson's garage for a while.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:29.970
Wart, you don't suppose when they get...

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:31.940
they'll try to float it, do you?

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:33.650
Now, June, relax.

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:35.490
I warned Wally not to be careless.

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:37.630
Anyway, if that boat sails anyplace,

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:39.850
it'll be in a vacant lot somewhere among the weeds.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:49.950
Get this far.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:51.920
Now let's get in the water, okay, Wally?

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:54.390
Yeah, well, okay.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:55.850
We should have brought our lunch.

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:58.140
We could take turns sailing around in it all day.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:07:59.920
Okay, odd man tries first.

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:02.530
One, two, three.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.420
One, two, three.

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:11.890
Well, Chester, I guess you're our first captain.

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:13.690
Yeah, Chester, you get in and we'll shove you off.

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:21.530
What's the matter?

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.700
Well, the bucket's too high. I can't squeeze in.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:28.900
Let's pull the bucket out.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:29.980
We can, it's...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.670
Nailed. If we pull the bucket out, we'll pull the bottom out.

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:36.630
Heck, I guess we're gonna have to take it back and try and fix it.

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:41.970
Yeah. Gee, I sure ain't till I get all the way back without even seeing how it works.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.670
Maybe we could pile some rocks in it and see how it floats.

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:46.470
Rocks aren't the same as a guy.

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:48.590
But we don't have a guy small enough.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:57.320
Hey, Beeb.

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:11.370
water's cold. So what? Give the shrimp the paddle. You all set? Okay. One, two, three, ship ahoy!

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:35.090
You okay, Beef?

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:39.770
Yeah, I'm okay. I'm just cool. That's all.

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:42.640
Why don't you do a crazy thing like tipping our boat over?

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:48.080
I just sat there. The boat did all the tipping over.

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:55.840
You know, we never should have brought that thing down here.

00:09:56.270 --> 00:09:57.790
You think we ought to try and fix it?

00:09:58.390 --> 00:09:59.970
Now, how are we going to get it out of the way?

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:02.730
Beaver's already wet. He could get it.

00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:05.310
Aw, cut it out. We gotta get him home.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:10.000
Oh, Ollie, my clothes are all wet.

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:11.840
Look at my new boots.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.300
You know something? I think Dad's gonna be real sore when he sees me.

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:19.600
I'll say. He told me to watch it.

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:20.970
At least he sees what happened.

00:10:21.190 --> 00:10:24.250
Maybe when you get home, your pop won't even notice he got wet.

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:44.330
I'll fix that heating vent in the dining room.

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:45.210
Any other little chores?

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:45.920
No.

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:47.900
In that case, I think I'll go out in the living room

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:49.320
and try and get some work done before lunch.

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:52.010
I wish I knew where those boys went with that boat.

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:54.870
Well, dear, I told you, they're probably over at Chester's.

00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:57.570
Now, Wally wouldn't try any nonsense without my permission.

00:10:58.490 --> 00:10:59.150
Oh, I don't know.

00:10:59.270 --> 00:10:59.970
I just picked up...

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.810
their room a little while ago and Bieber's hiking boots aren't in the closet now why would he wear

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:09.140
hiking boots to build a boat well if you're concerned why don't you call the Andersons

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:13.940
well if I do that and they're not there I'm going to worry mrs. Anderson and if they are there the

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:19.070
boys are going to think I'm fussing over them I'll wait till noontime when I have a good excuse

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:39.610
Beaver, you kind of smell like a swamp.

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:42.140
I feel like one, too.

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:47.630
Hey, Wally, is the coast clear?

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:50.410
Nah, my mom's in the kitchen and dad's in the living room.

00:11:51.830 --> 00:11:54.310
Boy, if they see Beaver messed up like this, we're sunk.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:59.960
If Mom wasn't in the kitchen, at least we could get in the house and maybe...

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:03.060
Hey, Wally, I got an idea.

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:06.400
I'll go across to my house and call your pop on the telephone.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:08.050
Now, what good would that do?

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:10.850
Well, I'll see I'm somebody that wants to talk to his mom.

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:12.390
That'll get her out of the kitchen.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:14.080
Ah, that's goofy.

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:15.810
Well, what else you gonna do?

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:19.130
Sit here till your pop comes out and catches him sitting here like a drowned rat?

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:23.740
Let's try it, Wally, even if it is goofy.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:27.100
Well, okay. Come on, sister.

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:29.150
Come out that way.

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:32.570
Keep it down.

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:46.600
Hey, Peeve, I'm sorry you got wet and everything.

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:48.650
That's okay, Wally.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:51.320
At least I got to be the first captain.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:55.410
Hey, you look kind of funny.

00:12:55.800 --> 00:12:57.160
You're not thinking of crying, are you?

00:12:57.830 --> 00:12:59.970
No, not unless...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.260
Once we get caught, then I think we're going to be in so much trouble.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:04.660
Why better?

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:19.300
What's that?

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:23.580
Oh, yes.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:31.160
Let me ask who's calling.

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:32.850
You want to know who's calling?

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:34.320
Just say it's a friend.

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:35.320
Hello?

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:36.060
Oh, hello?

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:38.640
Yeah, it's a friend of hers that you don't know.

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:43.690
Well, uh, uh, all right, just a moment.

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:44.370
I'll call her.

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:48.060
Hey, Chester, where?

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:48.860
He went to get her.

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:50.710
What should I do now?

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:52.400
Well, I think you better hang it up.

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:55.120
Oh, dear.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:13:58.180
Who is it?

00:13:58.240 --> 00:13:59.960
A friend of yours that I don't know.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:24.440
I think I recognized the voice.

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:26.460
Sounded like that little Phillips girl down the street.

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:27.600
Was always playing with the phone.

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:29.530
I'd like to speak to her mother.

00:14:35.150 --> 00:14:36.190
What's this?

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:40.500
That new survey we took down at the office on women's marketing habits.

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:41.650
Look it over.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:57.380
It's right in there.

00:14:58.130 --> 00:14:59.210
We'll never get upstairs.

00:15:01.370 --> 00:15:02.790
Wait a minute. I'm going to try something.

00:15:03.530 --> 00:15:11.860
I don't think some of these things are flattering at all.

00:15:12.460 --> 00:15:14.120
Oh, dear, I can't help the way women shop.

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:16.090
Who took the survey?

00:15:16.750 --> 00:15:18.030
Some of the fellows down at the office.

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:19.760
I thought so.

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:23.010
Well, not you, and that's got nothing to do with it.

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:26.470
Dad!

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:28.190
It sounds like the garbage disposal.

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:29.810
Well, how would it go on by itself?

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.280
Maybe it's a short circuit.

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:32.280
Open.

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:50.810
I don't know.

00:15:52.060 --> 00:15:52.940
I had a car once.

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:54.920
Every time you slammed the door, the lights went on.

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:15.150
When did you fellows come home?

00:16:15.830 --> 00:16:17.680
Uh, oh, before.

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:20.470
Say, you didn't bring that boat back here, did you?

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:22.670
Oh, no, sir, we sure didn't.

00:16:23.830 --> 00:16:25.510
Why don't I put this junk in the cellar?

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:29.190
Oh, hi, Mom.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:31.500
I didn't know you were home.

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:33.640
Um, Dad knows it.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:40.290
Gotta put some junk down in the cellar.

00:16:40.510 --> 00:16:42.970
Oh, you cleaning up your room?

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:46.380
Um, sorta.

00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:54.100
Wally, Wally, isn't that the bathtub in your room?

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:57.000
Um, yeah, Beavers taking a bath.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:16:59.960
On Saturday morning without anyone...

00:17:02.530 --> 00:17:06.020
Um, yeah, uh, he kind of took one look at his feet

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:07.660
and then decided maybe he ought to take one.

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:09.180
Oh.

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:11.950
Well, you better tell him to hurry up.

00:17:12.050 --> 00:17:12.790
We're gonna have lunch soon.

